::CW_U02_L06_Q01::[MLA.CW.NAR.02] Which sentence best slows an important moment?{
~A. I went home and did homework.#Incorrect. This compresses routine action. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. The week passed quickly.#Incorrect. This compresses time. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. The envelope bent under my thumb as I waited for the principal to say my name.#Correct. This expands the moment with detail and waiting.
~D. I left.#Incorrect. This is brief and fast. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_L06_Q02::[MLA.CW.NAR.02] What is pacing?{
=A. How fast or slow a scene feels to the reader#Correct. Pace controls the reader's sense of speed.
~B. The emotional atmosphere of a scene#Incorrect. That is mood. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. The narrator's attitude#Incorrect. That is tone. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. The person telling the story#Incorrect. That is the narrator. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_L06_Q03::[MLA.CW.NAR.02] Passage: "The latch clicked once, softly, and for a second I could hear only the rain ticking against the window." What effect does the pacing create?{
~A. Fast summary#Incorrect. The moment is expanded, not summarized. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Suspenseful delay#Correct. The sentence slows the moment and makes the reader wait.
~C. Comic exaggeration#Incorrect. The tone is not mainly comic. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. No mood#Incorrect. The rain and soft click create mood.
}

::CW_U02_L06_Q04::[MLA.CW.NAR.02] Which choice best creates a lonely mood without naming loneliness?{
~A. I was lonely.#Incorrect. This names the mood directly. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Loneliness is sad.#Incorrect. This is a general statement. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. The room was a room.#Incorrect. This creates no mood. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. One paper cup sat on the table, its rim bent inward like it had been waiting too long.#Correct. The image suggests loneliness. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_L06_Q05::[MLA.CW.NAR.02] When should a writer use compression?{
=A. When moving quickly through less important time or action#Correct. Compression saves space for important moments. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. When every small action needs full detail#Incorrect. That would slow the scene too much.
~C. Only when writing poetry#Incorrect. Compression is useful in narrative, too. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. To remove all meaning from a scene#Incorrect. Compression should support meaning. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_L06_Q06::[MLA.CW.NAR.02] Which passage creates tension through stakes?{
~A. I opened the box.#Incorrect. This has little context for stakes. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. I opened a box that was box-shaped.#Incorrect. This adds no stakes. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. I opened the box because the letter inside would decide whether we had to move.#Correct. The outcome matters, so tension increases. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. Boxes can be opened.#Incorrect. This is general information. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_L06_Q07::[MLA.CW.NAR.02] Which sentence has a playful tone?{
~A. The hallway light clicked twice, then held steady over the closed doors.#Incorrect. This creates an uneasy mood. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. The hallway light made one heroic attempt at drama before deciding to behave like a normal light.#Correct. The personified phrasing creates a playful attitude.
~C. The hallway was silent and gray.#Incorrect. This tone is more serious or bleak.
~D. The hallway was described.#Incorrect. This is vague. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_L06_Q08::[MLA.CW.NAR.02] Which sentence most clearly speeds up routine action?{
~A. I tied the left shoe, then the right shoe, then tightened both laces.#Incorrect. This slows routine action. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. The shoelace crossed the tongue of the shoe.#Incorrect. This expands a minor action. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. My right hand reached toward my left shoe.#Incorrect. This is too detailed for routine action.
=D. I got ready in three minutes and ran for the bus.#Correct. This compresses routine preparation. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_L06_Q09::[MLA.CW.NAR.02] What creates mood?{
=A. Setting, imagery, sensory detail, figurative language, and diction#Correct. These choices shape emotional atmosphere. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. Only the number of pages#Incorrect. Page count does not create mood by itself.
~C. Only the title#Incorrect. Titles may contribute, but mood is built through many craft choices.
~D. Only grammar labels#Incorrect. Grammar alone does not define mood. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_L06_Q10::[MLA.CW.NAR.02] Passage: "The clouds pressed low over the field like a lid." What does the figurative language create?{
~A. A light, carefree mood#Incorrect. The image feels heavy, not carefree. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. A heavy, trapped mood#Correct. The comparison to a lid suggests pressure and confinement.
~C. No mood at all#Incorrect. The image strongly shapes mood. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. A satirical target#Incorrect. This is mood through image, not satire.
}

::CW_U02_L06_Q11::[MLA.CW.NAR.02] Which revision best creates short-sentence impact?{
~A. The latch clicked once, and I heard many details for several lines before anything happened.#Incorrect. This slows and expands, not short-sentence impact.
~B. The latch was a latch.#Incorrect. This adds no effect. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. The latch clicked. I froze.#Correct. The short sentences create a sudden pause and impact.
~D. I went home later.#Incorrect. This is compressed but not impactful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_L06_Q12::[MLA.CW.NAR.02] Which statement correctly distinguishes mood and tone?{
=A. Mood is the emotional atmosphere; tone is the narrator's attitude.#Correct. Mood is what the scene feels like, while tone is attitude.
~B. Mood and tone are always identical.#Incorrect. They can be related but are not the same.
~C. Tone is only sentence length.#Incorrect. Tone includes attitude and word choice. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Mood is only punctuation.#Incorrect. Mood is created by many craft choices.
}

::CW_U02_L06_Q13::[MLA.CW.NAR.02] Passage: "The door opened. A hand reached through. The lights went out." What pacing effect is strongest?{
~A. Slow reflective summary#Incorrect. The passage is quick and action-focused.
~B. Playful comedy#Incorrect. The effect is tense rather than playful.
~C. No action#Incorrect. The passage has action. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Fast, tense movement through short sentences#Correct. The short sentences make the moment feel quick and tense.
}

::CW_U02_L06_Q14::[MLA.CW.NAR.02] Which feedback best helps revise for pacing?{
~A. Make it good.#Incorrect. This is too vague. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Slow down the moment when the character sees the message, then compress the walk home.#Correct. This gives specific pacing guidance. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. Add random adjectives everywhere.#Incorrect. Random detail does not control pace. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Delete all important moments.#Incorrect. Important moments need development. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_L06_Q15::[MLA.CW.NAR.02] Which detail creates a serious tone?{
~A. The narrator jokes that the hallway light is auditioning for a drama club.#Incorrect. That is playful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. The narrator says the hallway light was hilarious.#Incorrect. That is comic. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. The narrator describes the hallway as quiet, cold, and lined with closed doors.#Correct. The diction creates a serious tone. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. The narrator calls the door silly.#Incorrect. That is playful or humorous. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_L06_Q16::[MLA.CW.NAR.02] What is delay as a pacing technique?{
=A. Making the reader wait for information or outcome#Correct. Delay builds anticipation. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. Revealing everything immediately#Incorrect. That removes delay. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Removing stakes#Incorrect. Stakes help tension. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Changing every name#Incorrect. That is not pacing. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_L06_Q17::[MLA.CW.NAR.02] Which passage uses expansion?{
~A. The month passed.#Incorrect. This compresses time. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. I got ready and left.#Incorrect. This is compressed. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Later, everything happened.#Incorrect. This is summary, not expansion. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. I reached for the handle, stopped, wiped my palm on my jeans, and listened for footsteps.#Correct. The important moment is slowed with actions and sensory attention.
}

::CW_U02_L06_Q18::[MLA.CW.NAR.02] Which choice best creates an eerie mood?{
~A. The playground was fun and bright.#Incorrect. This creates a cheerful mood. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. The playground swings moved, though the air was still.#Correct. The image suggests something unsettling. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. The playground had equipment.#Incorrect. This is neutral and vague. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. The playground existed.#Incorrect. This creates no mood. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_L06_Q19::[MLA.CW.NAR.02] Which explanation is strongest?{
~A. The scene is tense because I say it is tense.#Incorrect. This gives no evidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. The writer uses words.#Incorrect. This is too vague. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. The writer slows the moment with three small actions before the door opens, which creates tension because the reader waits for what is inside.#Correct. This names the technique and explains effect.
~D. The scene has punctuation.#Incorrect. Punctuation alone is not enough explanation. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_L06_Q20::[MLA.CW.NAR.02] Which sentence uses compression appropriately?{
=A. For three ordinary weeks, nothing changed.#Correct. This quickly moves through unimportant time. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. The important confession happened in one vague sentence.#Incorrect. Important moments usually need more development. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. I opened the dangerous box with no detail at all.#Incorrect. A high-stakes moment may need expansion.
~D. The final choice was skipped.#Incorrect. Skipping the key moment weakens the scene.
}

::CW_U02_L06_Q21::[MLA.CW.NAR.02] Which phrase best describes tension?{
~A. A list of character names#Incorrect. Names alone do not create tension. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Pressure, uncertainty, conflict, or anticipation#Correct. Tension makes the reader wait for an outcome.
~C. The exact font size#Incorrect. Font size is not narrative tension. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. A grammar rule only#Incorrect. Tension is a reader effect. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_L06_Q22::[MLA.CW.NAR.02] Which revision best changes "I was scared" into mood and tension?{
~A. I was very scared.#Incorrect. This still tells the feeling. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Fear happened.#Incorrect. This is vague. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Scared scared scared.#Incorrect. Repetition alone does not create a clear scene.
=D. The floorboard creaked behind me, and I stopped breathing long enough to hear it creak again.#Correct. Sound, action, and delay create fear. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_L06_Q23::[MLA.CW.NAR.02] Which choice best identifies tone in this sentence? "Naturally, the one day I needed the printer, it decided to become a decorative sculpture."{
~A. Solemn and ceremonial#Incorrect. The sentence is humorous. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Neutral and scientific#Incorrect. The diction is not neutral. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Sarcastic or humorous#Correct. The narrator's attitude is shown through "Naturally" and the printer becoming a "decorative sculpture."
~D. Terrified#Incorrect. The tone is frustration or humor, not terror.
}

::CW_U02_L06_Q24::[MLA.CW.NAR.02] What should a writer do when a scene feels flat?{
~A. Add unrelated events.#Incorrect. Random events do not create controlled effect.
~B. Remove all sensory detail.#Incorrect. Sensory detail can build mood and tension.
~C. Keep the flat version unchanged.#Incorrect. Revision is needed. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Decide the intended effect, then revise pace, detail, diction, imagery, or sentence length to create it.#Correct. Revision should target reader experience. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_L06_Q25::[MLA.CW.NAR.02] Which checkpoint explanation meets mastery?{
=A. I slowed the moment when the phone lit up by adding two actions and a reflection in the screen. This creates tension because the reader waits to see whether the narrator will answer.#Correct. This names pacing choices and explains effect.
~B. I made it tense because it is tense.#Incorrect. This gives no craft evidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. I wrote a scene.#Incorrect. This does not explain pacing or effect.
~D. I used a character name.#Incorrect. Character naming is not enough for pacing mastery.
}
